The rewrite caches the message's field pointer and field count in locals. This avoids reloading them through `mesg` on each loop iteration. Behavior is unchanged: it still returns the index of the first matching field, or `FIT_UINT16_INVALID`. The gain is probably small, because at -O2 the compiler may already keep these values in registers. I left the `GetMesg` lookup itself alone, since it relies on profile internals not shown here.

```cpp
const FIT_UINT16 Profile::GetFieldIndex(const FIT_UINT16 mesgNum, const FIT_UINT8 fieldNum)
{
    const MESG *mesg = GetMesg(mesgNum);

    if (mesg == NULL)
        return FIT_UINT16_INVALID;

    const FIELD *fields = mesg->fields;
    const FIT_UINT16 n = mesg->numFields;

    for (FIT_UINT16 i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (fields[i].num == fieldNum)
            return i;
    }

    return FIT_UINT16_INVALID;
}
```